WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.140
SPARSH KESARI: Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.600
Welcome to Microsoft Build 2026.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.260
I am Sparsh Kesari, Developer
Relations Manager at TestMu AI,

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.620
and we are very excited
to be back here.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.620
Today, I am going to talk about
a very interesting topic,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.600
agentic testing, because here we
are, agents can write code now.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.390
They can also run it,
break it, and patch it.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:26.100
What they haven't really able to
do is test it, not in the way

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.190
that you would trust before
shipping an application

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.190
to production, and
that's the gap.

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:35.670
An agent can generate
hundreds of codes in seconds,

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.720
but the speed isn't
the hard part.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.700
The hard part is trust.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.800
Did it test the right thing?

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.260
Can you actually rely
on what it tells you

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.850
when it says everything passed?

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.950
That's what I want
to talk about today.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.250
Specifically, I want to
show you our new Kane CLI,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.010
the newest product
launched by TestMu AI

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:00.600
to help ship end-to-end
products in an agentic era.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.470
It is a command line tool built
so that agents can test the code

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:08.400
in a careful, ingenious way,
not just running checks,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.040
but giving you something that
you can actually rely on.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.340
Everyone is focused on how fast
we are building, but very few

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.770
of us are asking
the real question,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.960
how fast are we validating?

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.500
How are we able to trust an
application that was built

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.140
by a set of autonomous
agents and deployed

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.090
by another set of agents?

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.410
How are we able to trust
a feature or a page

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.740
that was developed by an agent
is doing what it is supposed

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.490
to be doing without any
human intervention?

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.940
This is the exact blind spot
that we are targeting today.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.420
The fix isn't adding more a
intelligent, smarter system,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.600
but it's to add a
deterministic validation layer

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.650
between shipping
and development.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.000
To solve this, we at TestMu
AI released Kane CLI.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.510
Kane CLI is a command
line validation layer

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:09.090
that helps you test any feature
application on a local browser.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.860
Kane CLI can be used by humans
and directly by agents.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.130
It natively integrates
with all of your agents.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.820
It could be Claude Code,
Codex, Gemini, Copilot,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.550
etc. You can describe to
Kane CLI what you want

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.350
in natural language prompts,

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:30.180
and Kane CLI will open a local
browser, test your feature out,

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.990
and you also have the option
to validate that on Cloud,

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.990
on different cross-browser,
cross-device,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.720
cross-environment setups.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.870
This helps you
release your features

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.460
and products with confidence.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:50.010
What do you get out of
the box with Kane CLI?

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.960
First is intent-based control.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.760
You just need to describe

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.400
to Kane CLI the natural
language objectives.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.370
There is no brittle test
scripts, no locators,

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.400
no selectors, no exports.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.490
Kane CLI also helps
with resilient runs.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.380
It stays on the task even

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.200
when there is a complex
journey involved.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.280
Kane CLI also automatically
discovers bugs while it is

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.700
executing and if
there is any failure.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.440
It has vision-based waiting.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.160
It actually waits on the
required rendered page,

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.400
or feature than adding
arbitrary time waits.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:33.120
Kane CLI, by default, generates
the Playwright code of the test

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.080
that is created, so you can put
in the natural language prompts,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.760
and the Playwright test
case comes out back.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.900
It also has a test.md framework
which has all the test cases

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.020
that we have written
in a markdown value.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.080
You can auto play it anytime
and you can also trigger it

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.430
by your CI/CD pipeline.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:59.970
All the test cases generated by
Kane CLI also have Autoheals.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.610
If there is any UI
change on your page,

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:05.100
Kane CLI will automatically
heal the test cases

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.830
and give you the new
test code in return.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.100
You also have
shareable evidence.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.790
You have video logs as well
as trace runs for each run.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.800
Kane CLI also gives out
agent-native NDJSON format,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.000
so it's a structured output

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.400
which is understandable
by all the agents.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.070
To use Kane CLI, we
have three options.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.130
You can directly use in
your command line CLI

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.520
by running this command,
and just add your prompts

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.230
in natural language prompts.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.990
Kane CLI will start
automating that.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:47.190
We also have an SDK where
you can just import Kane

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.970
from our SDK and which can
be used in your CI pipeline

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:53.940
or any custom agent
or any of your tests.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.540
Kane CLI can also automatically
talk to your agent tool.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:01.410
Just point your agent
to our agent.md file,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.970
and your agent will
understand how

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.270
to use Kane CLI
effectively while building

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.970
your applications.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.450
Let's see this in action.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:13.580
To start using Kane
CLI, you just need

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:17.630
to download the npm
module in your system.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:20.210
You can directly copy
this command and paste

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:25.320
in the terminal and let your
Kane CLI get downloaded.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.130
Once downloaded, you can open
any terminal and call Kane CLI

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.440
to start running
your test cases.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.680
For now, I will open Kane
CLI in our interactive mode.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.260
The session gets initialized,
and we get an option

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:42.780
to paste our objective.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.090
I can write any kind
of objective here

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.480
and Kane CLI will start
completing that workflow.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.890
For now, I have a sample
website where we are trying

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:56.970
to complete the checkout flow
and pasting the order ID

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:02.580
from our first session into
Google in another session.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.400
Kane CLI will start
understanding the objective,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.930
and as you can see, it has
bifurcated into two workflows.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.380
The first task is to
complete the checkout flow,

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.750
and the second task is
to copy the order ID

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.100
and paste it in another session.

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:23.160
To complete this, Kane CLI
will open a local browser

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.490
and start completing and
thinking about the next steps.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:27.120
As you can see,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.030
it has automatically
opened the sample website

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.140
that we have added, and it is
thinking how it can navigate

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.800
to the next step.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.050
You can also see all of the
steps that it has identified

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.140
in the terminal itself,

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.110
and it will take some
time to complete this.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.970
Let's also check
out another method

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:05.890
where you can use Kane CLI
directly with your agents.

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:12.600
To do this, you just need
to paste this agent.md file

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.870
to your agent, and your
agent will understand how

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.830
to use Kane CLI while it is
building out a new feature.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.380
For this, let's open another
browser and call out Claude.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:37.470
To call Kane CLI within
your agent browser,

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:40.860
you can just use it by
directly adding a slash

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.450
and typing "Kane CLI."

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:47.070
A Kane CLI session
will be initialized

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:50.160
within your agent environment.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.740
It is identifying my user ID,
and Kane CLI is ready to use.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:59.550
As you can see, you will also
see what are the different types

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:01.860
of actions Kane CLI can perform?

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.620
It can perform any type
of actions, assertions;

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:07.470
it can extract any
type of top results

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.430
and also run any test
suite that we already have

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:15.450
within our project file.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.880
For this, let's try
something simple.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:29.440
"Go to testmuai.com and
validate if the page opens."

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.330
Kane CLI directly interacts
with your AI agent

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:42.450
and thinks about the next steps.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.480
While you are building with
any of your AI agents,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.680
let's say it is Claude,
Codex, or Copilot,

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.410
your agent can directly
define what kind

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.250
of end-to-end test cases it
wants to write according

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:57.830
to the feature or product
that you are developing,

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:00.950
and Kane CLI will provide
them hands and eyes

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:05.010
to perform those
end-to-end workflows.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:11.820
By default, for any agent,
it runs in a headless mode,

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.580
and you will see all
the results popping

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:17.470
up here once this
step is completed.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.550
Meanwhile, let's
go back and check

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.490
out what is happening
in our UI mode.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:27.960
As you can see, it is placing
an order and also thinking

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:29.880
about adding a --
as you can see,

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:33.420
it can also automatically
generate some test data

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.790
to complete the card
checkout process.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.860
It has completed the order,

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:48.150
and it has almost
completed the first flow

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:50.070
that it has identified.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:51.870
Now, it is extracting
the order ID

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:55.810
from the first
flow confirmation.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.920
Now, it has completed
the first flow.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:08.610
It has extracted and saved the
order ID from our first flow,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:12.420
and now it will try to
complete our second step.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:15.150
That is, opening google.com
and pasting the order ID

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.130
from our first test case.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:28.450
Now, it has opened google.com,

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:33.820
and it will now
paste our order ID.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:48.540
It has successfully pasted the
order ID from our first flow

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:57.390
into the second flow, and it
has completed the workflow.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.340
It has passed the test case.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.320
Now, once my test
case is completed,

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.120
I can click on "Exit,"

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.590
and it will save the session

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:11.020
into the particular file
folder that we have wanted.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.690
It will also download a
created Playwright script

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.330
of the test case that
we have completed,

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:25.590
and it will also show
it a shareable link.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:31.930
I can just directly copy this
and open it in my browser.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:42.270
Here, we get all the logs of the
steps that we have completed.

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:44.850
I can automatically
autoplay this,

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:50.130
and it will play how
Kane CLI, basically,

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:51.480
completed this workflow.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:53.400
You can also see the cursor,

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.350
what kind of decisions
it is making,

00:11:55.350 --> 00:12:00.160
and how it is completing
the user's objective,

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:06.540
and this becomes a shareable
proof of any step,

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:09.000
any test cases that
we are running.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.900
You can validate it
before completing,

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:17.980
before merging this feature
or product into your --

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:29.310
If there are any bugs that are
identified within this step,

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:33.840
you will also see Kane
CLI automatically marking

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:36.930
that bug to the user as well.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:40.710
Now, let's also see what
happened while using Kane CLI

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:43.300
with our AI agent.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:48.510
Our AI agent ran the
objective in a headless mode,

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:51.870
and it gave not many stats.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:55.170
The test case was passed,
the objective was completed,

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.930
and it also gave us
an agent rating.

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:00.990
You can also see the shareable
proof by copying this

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:04.840
and pasting and viewing
it in your own browser.
